<% content_for :title, "Privacy — Openstage" %>
<% content_for :main_class, "os-wrap os-stack pt-1.5" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "What Openstage counts on builders' websites and its own pages, and what it keeps." %>

<section class="os-panel p-6 sm:p-12">
  <span class="os-tag">Privacy</span>
  <h1 class="os-h1 mt-4 max-w-[900px]">What Openstage counts, and what it keeps.</h1>
  <p class="os-p mt-4.5 max-w-[720px] text-lg">Openstage counts visits so builders can see who reads their work. It counts people in totals, never as profiles.</p>
  <p class="os-xs os-muted mt-3">Updated October 28, 2026.</p>
</section>

<section id="builders-sites" class="os-panel scroll-mt-6" aria-labelledby="on-builders-sites">
  <h2 id="on-builders-sites" class="os-h2">On a builder's website</h2>
  <div class="mt-3 max-w-[720px] space-y-3.5">
    <p class="os-p">
      A builder can add Openstage's script to their project's website to count its visits. On each page you view there, it
      sends Openstage the page's address and the project's code; the request carries your IP address and browser name,
      as every request does. It sets no cookie and stores nothing in your browser.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">
      Openstage adds the view to that day's totals for the project: page views, and visitors, a visitor being one browser
      on one day. To count you once a day, it keeps a hash of your IP address made with a key that changes every day,
      and deletes it within two days. It does not keep the address, the page, where you came from, or your device.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">
      Views from bots, views from any site other than the project's own, and views while the project's counting is off
      or paused are not counted. The builder sees the totals, and the project's page on Openstage can show them.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="os-panel" aria-labelledby="on-openstage">
  <h2 id="on-openstage" class="os-h2">On Openstage's own pages</h2>
  <div class="mt-3 max-w-[720px] space-y-3.5">
    <p class="os-p">
      A visit to a builder's page or a project's page records the time, the site you came from, your country as
      Cloudflare reports it, and a hash of your IP address with the date, not the address itself. A click on a page's
      links records the time and the same hash. Bots and the builder's own visits are left out. Builders on Pro see
      these numbers.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">Openstage's own site analytics run on Umami, which sets no cookie.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="os-panel" aria-labelledby="if-you-sign-in">
  <h2 id="if-you-sign-in" class="os-h2">If you sign in, follow or pay</h2>
  <div class="mt-3 max-w-[720px] space-y-3.5">
    <p class="os-p">
      Signing in with GitHub gives Openstage your GitHub username, name, avatar and email, and a token to read your
      repositories' commits, which it stores encrypted. Deleting your account in Settings removes it for good after 30 days.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">
      Following a builder or a project keeps your email. Nothing but the confirmation is sent until you confirm, and
      every email after it has a one-click unsubscribe.
    </p>
    <p class="os-p">Payments go through Stripe; Openstage never sees your card.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="os-panel" aria-labelledby="questions">
  <h2 id="questions" class="os-h2">Questions</h2>
  <p class="os-p mt-2.5 max-w-[720px]">
    Write to <a href="mailto:tuxnotfound@cioga.eu" class="os-link">tuxnotfound@cioga.eu</a>.
  </p>
</section>
